{
  "id": "c6",
  "title": "for Loops",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c6-l1",
      "title": "The for Loop",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Most loops count: start, condition, step. for puts all three in one line: for (i = 1; i <= 5; i++). It's the same as a while loop, just tidier.",
        "The first part runs once, the condition is checked before each round, and the step runs after each round.",
        "Kernighan and Ritchie's book has a famous early example: a table that converts Fahrenheit to Celsius, made with a for loop."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int fahr;",
          "    for (fahr = 0; fahr <= 100; fahr = fahr + 20)",
          "        printf(\"%3d %6.1f\\n\", fahr, (5.0 / 9.0) * (fahr - 32));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "  0  -17.8",
          " 20   -6.7",
          " 40    4.4",
          " 60   15.6",
          " 80   26.7",
          "100   37.8"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c6-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In for (i = 0; i < 3; i++), how many rounds are there?",
          "options": [
            "3",
            "2",
            "4",
            "0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "i is 0, 1 and 2. At 3, i < 3 is false."
        },
        {
          "id": "c6-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "When does the step part i++ run?",
          "options": [
            "After each round",
            "Before each round",
            "Once at the start",
            "Only at the end"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Start once, then: check, body, step, check, body, step …"
        },
        {
          "id": "c6-l1-e3",
